zscot_reasoning:
The N stage for colon adenocarcinoma is determined based on the number of positive lymph nodes and the size of the largest metastasis. According to the AJCC Cancer Staging Manual (7th edition), 

N0: No regional lymph node metastasis;
N1: Metastasis in 1 to 3 regional lymph nodes;
N2: Metastasis in 4 or more regional lymph nodes;
N3: Metastasis in regional lymph nodes beyond the pericolic or perirectal lymph nodes.

In this case, the pathology report states that 10 out of 11 lymph nodes were involved. Therefore, the N stage is N2.

The T stage is already provided as pT4c/d, which refers to the primary tumor invading through the colon wall and perforating the visceral peritoneum.

So, the final stage for this patient is pT4c/d, N2.
